explainability of the AV’s behavior have been shown to be effective tools to foster trust and situation awareness in automated driving modes, which in turn can be seen as a prerequisite to a positive user experience and the establishment of the general acceptance of automated driving [28].
In this paper, we focus on the context of high safety- and timecritical situations, where trust in automation and situation awareness are of particular importance, as wrong decisions or automation failures can lead to (fatal) accidents [24]. To ensure trust, effective and reassuring communication between the human and the automated vehicle is essential. Passengers must be provided with sufficient information about the state of the vehicle, its environment, and perceptions, as well as its current and planned behavior at all times [15]. To support this, we investigated the influence of visual feedback on trust in automation and situation awareness in a safety-critical and unexpected driving situation in a mixed-method user study (N = 24).
2 RELATED WORK
2.1 Trust and Situation Awareness as Human Factor Challenges of Automated Driving
Trust in automation is a central human factor that will affect the willingness of users to adapt to automated vehicles. Lee and See [13] defined trust in automation as’ the attitude that an agent will help achieve an individual’s goals in a situation characterized by uncertainty and vulnerability’. Research has shown that users tend to fail to rely on automation on an appropriate level and often either assume the automation’s capabilities as too high (overtrust) or too low (distrust) [13]. In the context of automated driving, especially overtrust has already led to multiple accidents in which users overestimated the capabilities of automated driving functions[24]. Situation awareness is another important aspect in regard to automated vehicles and defined as “a sufficient knowledge about the vehicle‘s surroundings, the current state of the automation, the system‘s task performance, and the driver‘s own tasks and responsibilities” [8]. Although situation awareness is of particular relevance in the case of an upcoming authority transition as in level 3 automation [17], it is also crucial in situations of continuous automation (level 4 and 5), as unexpected driving maneuvers can startle the human passenger and lead to irritation, anxiety, and a negative impact on trust in the automated vehicle [25].
2.2 The Importance of Transparency and Explainability in Automated Driving
The most promising approach to taking up the presented human factor challenges of automated driving is to provide transparency and explainability of the AV’s behavior. Existing research on explainable automotive interfaces already provided an overview of explanations in autonomous driving [16] and highlighted the basic need for providing explanations about automated vehicle behavior [25] and the positive impact on situation awareness [26]. In terms of critical situations, collected data suggests that with fully automated vehicles (level 5), visualization of intention and explanation is useful and necessary, at least in the initial phase [3]. Existing research on explainable interfaces is often based on driving situations in which

