it is suitable to provide the explanation in a linguistically expressed manner as voice alerts [6, 10]. However, in highly-critical and unexpected driving situations like automated braking, there might not be enough time to provide the explanation within a sufficient time frame that allows the human driver to cognitively process the given information.
2.3 Augmented Reality Displays as a Means to Provide Information
Head-up displays (HUDs) are increasingly used as a solution to provide drivers with information without making them glance away from the road. Compared to in-car displays, they offer a higher situation awareness by remaining low in cognitive load [2]. Although the majority of research resulted in positive evaluations for AR displays and visualizations, recent research also found the possibility of negative results for AR visualizations, where they negatively impacted the impression of the AV’s capabilities and also supported overtrust [4]. Not only the timing, but also the amount of information and the way they are presented have to be carefully considered [1], as interpersonal differences in the desire for feedback and explanations were identified and some drivers can be annoyed [22] or cognitively overloaded, which in terms lead to lower acceptance and usability [18]. This highlights the importance of the AR concept evaluation for a variety of different driving contexts- and situations.
3 RESEARCH QUESTIONS AND HYPOTHESES
To address the research gap for time- and safety-critical driving situations, we built on the existing literature and aimed to test what kind of explainable interfaces are comprehensible enough to convey trust and situation awareness yet can be processed cognitively in a short time. To do this, we explored the research question of how much information should be presented in a safety-critical and unexpected situation to (1) generate trust in automation and (2) maintain and increase situation awareness. To this end, we derived the following hypotheses:
H1 Trust in automation is affected by displaying explainable information in the AR windshield display in safety-critical situations.
H2 Situation awareness is affected by displaying explainable information in the AR windshield display in safety-critical situations.
4 METHOD
4.1 Scenario and HMI Design
In order to investigate the formulated research question, a mixedmethod user study was conducted, consisting of a short virtual reality (VR) driving simulation experiment and a semi-structured interview. Participants experienced two windshield display concepts in a safety-critical highway traffic scene in the form of short 360° videos and a baseline condition without any information (see Figure 1). The VR videos had a duration of 20 seconds and were made with the free open-source 3D computer graphics software toolset Blender and presented with the HTC Vive VR headset. In

