the videos, the ego vehicle is driving in the middle lane of a threelane highway in an activated automation mode and the participant didn’t need to intervene in the driving task and had no interaction option to do so. Another car next to the ego vehicle on the right lane then tries to overtake a truck and pulls over into the ego vehicle’s lane without indicating the lane change. The ego-vehicle brakes and changes to the left lane in order to avoid a collision. This scenario was chosen since situations like these frequently occur in highway traffic. The high speed and the unpredictability of other traffic actors’ behavior make this a suitable representative of unexpected and safety-critical driving situations. Due to the dynamic nature of the scene, with the high speeds involved, we chose the visual modality for communication between automation and human. Using an AR windshield display allows for displaying information in the line of sight of the driver, thus not diverting his gaze away from the traffic scene in front. For the participants, this method makes it possible to parse and process the presented information fast enough in the dynamic scene. AR windshield displays have the potential to improve user trust, and acceptance in AVs [27] and also support the driver in anticipating lane change maneuvers [12]. To define the appropriate level of information, two concepts with different levels of information were visualized as shown in Figure 2. We categorized the amount of presented information into how and why information as used by Koo et al. [10]. In the first concept (concept B) only information about the vehicle’s own behavior is displayed. For this, the vehicle’s planned trajectory is visualized by a white line. In the second concept (concept C) the vehicle’s planned trajectory is visualized together with the trajectory of the overtaking vehicle from the right, which is highlighted in red to refer to the situation’s criticality. To convey safety, the left lane is marked in blue. This indicates that the lane is free and a lane change is possible without any risk (see Figure 2). To compare these HMI concepts to a baseline condition, a scenario with no AR visualizations (concept A) was implemented as well. In regard to the level of driving automation and the vehicle’s capabilities, automation according to SAE level 4 or 5 was chosen for the study, where the automation would handle the situation on itself and no take-over or intervention would be requested from the driver [17].
4.2 Dependent Variables
Trust in automation was measured with the Situational Trust Scale for Automated Driving (STS-AD) by Holthausen and Wintersberger et al. [9]. We chose this 7-point Likert scale questionnaire since it is specifically designed for the context of automated driving and addresses various aspects of situational trust. Situation awareness was measured with the Subjective Information Processing Awareness Scale (SIPAS) by Schrills [23]. This 6-point Likert scale is intended for contexts in which people work together with intelligent systems. Furthermore, the SIPA scale is particularly suitable for comparing different user interfaces with each other as intended in this study [23]. In the second part of the study, a semi-structured interview was conducted to allow for a detailed discussion and evaluation of the presented concepts and to gain further insight into the influencing factors of trust and situation awareness in critical traffic situations. To assess the understandability of the concepts, the participants were asked to assign the meaning of each object in the

