5.2 Qualitative Results (Interview)
In the Interview, the two explainable concepts were shown to the participants again and then discussed in detail. Most of the participants (>75%) understood the meaning of the white, red, and blue lines in the concepts. Concept C (how & why information) was rated as the most understandable concept by a clear majority of the participants (96%) and also was reported to induce the highest trust in automation (95%). Some participants (8.7%) also noted that being aware of all the recognized objects increases their trust in the automated vehicle. All participants stated that the baseline concept A, with no information at all, was not understandable enough and didn’t support trust. For daily usage, n = 19 participants would prefer using concept C, and n = 5 participants using concept B.
6 DISCUSSION
The results show that communicating information about the egovehicle, as well as other involved traffic objects can have a positive impact on the human’s trust in the automated vehicle (H1) and also the situation awareness (H2). Thus both hypotheses can be accepted. In regard to trust in automation, the STS-AD questionnaire and the results of the interview were consistent and revealed a strong preference for concept C (how & why information) in comparison with concept B (how information) and also the baseline concept A with no information. From a descriptive perspective, we can conclude that in this study, the more information was visualized, the higher the participants rated their trust in the automated vehicle. The same effect was shown for situation awareness. All concepts differ significantly in their ability to raise situation awareness, with concept C achieving the highest scores. As pointed out in section 2, situation awareness is of particular importance in contexts that require the authority to transition from the vehicle to the driver. This raises the question if we even need to consider situation awareness in full automation modes, where the driver has no obligation or even chance of intervening in the driving task. Although maybe not necessary when considered in isolation, situation awareness must be evaluated in the context of other human factor constructs and their dependencies. Previous research showed that situation awareness promotes and also moderates the impact of trust in automation [19], which is in line with the results in this work, where a clear correlation between the two constructs was revealed. Therefore, we recommend promoting situation awareness even in highly and fully automated driving and encouraging the use of augmented reality windshield displays as a suitable medium. Furthermore, it can be discussed how the driver’s engagement in a non-driving-related task might have influenced the results. When reading or using mobile devices, the driver is distracted, and a visual-only HMI concept might be overseen. On the other side, an auditory concept can be perceived as disturbing as well, so we would recommend, when accompanying the visual information with cues of other modalities,

