to do this only in a supplementary and unobtrusive way. While the results of this study might lead towards the conclusion that more explainable information is beneficial and will, in any case, improve situation awareness and trust, we have to interpret our work in the context of existing literature. Contrary to our results, other studies showed a higher level of information could also lead to a cognitive overload, and a negative user experience [5, 11, 18].
7 FUTURE WORK
The captured measures in this study showed strong results, yet they refer to the specific evaluation of two windshield concept in one example situation. Therefore, these findings cannot be generalized to the multitude of safety-critical situations that can occur in automated driving scenarios. Similar concepts may hold great potential, but future research should explore ways to increase the scalability of such concepts. Also, the short duration (22 seconds) of the presented scenarios could be seen as a limitation of this study. The participants’ immersion in the driving scene might have been higher if there would have been an extension of the driving scene before and after the critical situation. While the short scenarios didn’t seem to influence the subjective evaluation of the concepts, for future studies we recommend extending the driving scene and including non-driving related tasks to ensure validity and a more realistic driving experience.
8 CONCLUSION
The presented study investigated the use of AR windshield visualizations as a means to provide transparency and explainability in safety-critical automated driving situations in order to raise trust in automation and situation awareness. We chose a common yet critical highway overtaking scenario and conducted a mixed-methods user study consisting of a VR driving simulation experiment and a semi-structured interview. Two explainable concepts with varying degrees of information were compared against a baseline condition with no information. The results showed significantly higher trust and situation awareness scores under the presentation of the explainable concepts with the highest rating and preference for the concept with both information about the ego vehicle’s behavior as well as other affected traffic objects. In the presented study, AR windshield visualizations were an accepted and unobtrusive means of assisting drivers in the safety-critical situation and we encourage the research community to investigate similar concepts in other situations of unexpecting driving behavior to allow for safe and enjoyable automated driving experiences.
